{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"pernicious-placid-kitten","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Advisory plan-review nudge: flag a leaf whose criteria count strains a single completion-verification run\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nTicket `d59e-e042-2b94-4b70` fixes the load-bearing problem: the completion verifier's budgets were\nflat constants that did not scale, so a large ticket could not be verified at all. That fix is the\nreal solution and this ticket must never become a prerequisite for it.\n\nThis is the **belt-and-suspenders** complement: reduce how often a ticket reaches the size where\nverification is strained in the first place, by coaching the author at plan time. Measured\nmotivation from d59e: ticket `9fd4-a94c-156e-4a56` carries **14 acceptance criteria**, and the\nprimary verifier had a FLAT 480-iteration budget for all of them (~34 tool calls per criterion),\nexhausting after ~780s. A verifier must produce one verdict record per criterion\n(`completion_recovery._validate_coverage`), so verification effort is linear in criteria count —\na fact no plan-review criterion currently surfaces to the author.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd a new **advisory, never-blocking** criterion in the decomposition-shape family, modelled\ndirectly on the existing `decomp-shape` criterion (`docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md:599`), which\nis already documented \"Advisory — never blocks\". It fires on a **leaf** ticket whose acceptance\ncriteria count exceeds a configured threshold, and coaches the author to split the work into\nindependently verifiable children rather than one ticket the verifier must cover in a single pass.\n\nMake the check **deterministic** (a count of `- [ ]` entries in the `## Acceptance Criteria`\nblock), so it runs in the existing deterministic floor with no LLM cost and no added latency —\nconsistent with how the other cheap structural checks run.\n\n**Explicitly non-load-bearing.** The criterion is advisory: it never blocks a claim, and d59e's\nbudget scaling remains the mechanism that guarantees any ticket can be verified. If this criterion\nwere ever promoted to blocking it would reintroduce exactly the failure mode d59e removes — an\nauthor unable to proceed because a ticket is large — so its advisory status is itself an\nacceptance criterion below.\n\n**Threshold.** Default to firing above **12** criteria on a leaf, with the value config-overridable\nalongside the other plan-review tunables rather than hardcoded. 12 sits above the repo's typical\nleaf and below 9fd4's 14, so it would have coached the ticket that motivated this without firing on\nordinary work. Assume this default unless measurement later says otherwise.\n\n**Alternative rejected — make it blocking.** That would trade a verifier availability defect for a\nplan-time roadblock, and would stop an author writing a genuinely large-but-atomic ticket. The\nwhole point of d59e is that size must not prevent progress.\n\n**Alternative rejected — score description length instead of criteria count.** Length is a weak\nproxy: the verifier's work is driven by the number of verdict records it must produce, not by prose\nsize, and a long Context section costs the verifier almost nothing. Criteria count is the signal\nthat actually tracks verification effort.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/det_floor.py` — the deterministic count check and its threshold\n  constant.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/criteria_routing.json` — register the criterion and route it to the\n  leaf/decomposition scope, advisory tier.\n- `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` — the generated per-criterion section.\n- `tests/unit/` — a new test module for the criterion's fire/silence behaviour.\n\n## Testing\n\nDeterministic unit tests only — the check makes no LLM call, so no live tier is needed.\n\n- fires on a leaf with more criteria than the threshold, and the finding names the count;\n- stays SILENT at exactly the threshold (boundary), on a container (which delegates to children),\n  and on the exempt ticket types;\n- the finding is emitted in the advisory bucket, and a test asserts a plan carrying it still\n  yields a PASS verdict that signs an attestation — the anti-regression guard against it ever\n  becoming a roadblock;\n- the threshold is read from config rather than a literal, asserted by overriding it.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] a new advisory criterion fires on a LEAF ticket whose `## Acceptance Criteria` block contains\n      more than the configured threshold of `- [ ]` entries, and its finding text states the actual\n      count and suggests splitting into independently verifiable children\n- [ ] the criterion NEVER blocks: a test asserts a plan that triggers it still returns a PASS\n      verdict and still signs a claim attestation\n- [ ] the criterion is silent at exactly the threshold, on containers, and on the ticket types the\n      criteria registry already exempts — one test per case\n- [ ] the check is deterministic (no LLM call): a test asserts it produces its finding with the LLM\n      runner unavailable\n- [ ] the threshold is configuration-driven, not a hardcoded literal at the call site, asserted by\n      a test that overrides it and observes the fire/silence boundary move\n- [ ] `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` documents the criterion, including that it is advisory\n      and never blocks, and states its relationship to `d59e` (this is a nudge, not the guarantee)\n- [ ] running the new criterion against ticket `9fd4-a94c-156e-4a56` (14 criteria) produces the\n      advisory finding, and against a 3-criterion leaf produces nothing — recorded as a comment","id":"259c-0f29-e920-4fab","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Advisory plan-review nudge: flag a leaf whose criteria count strains one completion-verification run"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785451278088297001,"uuid":"7b4307de-2368-4c73-b63e-7c32da0d29b0"}